144
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:31,940
But still, to get this kind of result from just a single prompt...

145
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:33,490
And I'm already pretty satisfied with that.

146
00:07:33,490 --> 00:07:35,290
Finally, let's take a look at a full-stack web application that 3.7-Max helped me build in just over ten minutes.

147
00:07:35,290 --> 00:07:39,140
It generated a full-stack web application for me in just over ten minutes.

148
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:43,400
Now, the most impressive part of this project isn't actually the front-end page.

149
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:48,050
but the database, backend APIs, and business logic that run behind it.

150
00:07:48,050 --> 00:07:51,830
I built this application using 3.7-Max in OpenCode.

151
00:07:51,830 --> 00:07:54,390
The entire process took just over ten minutes.

152
00:07:54,390 --> 00:08:00,290
But during those ten-plus minutes, the AI wasn't just slapping together a few buttons and pages.

153
00:08:00,290 --> 00:08:02,070
What really took the time,

154
00:08:02,070 --> 00:08:06,600
was building the backend structure that a real business system requires.

155
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:10,300
This project is called the Community Festival Booth Planning System.

156
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:13,060
On the surface, it looks like a simple booth map.

157
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:15,660
But behind the scenes, there's a complete data flow.

158
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:20,250
The stall owner submits an application, and the data is entered into the database.

159
00:08:20,250 --> 00:08:22,870
The administrator logs into the backend to review and approve it.

160
00:08:22,870 --> 00:08:25,830
Once approved, the stall can be assigned.

161
00:08:25,830 --> 00:08:31,500
During the assignment, the system also checks for any location conflicts, and whether the spot is near a power and water source.

162
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:34,089
and whether it's too close to the audience area.

163
00:08:34,089 --> 00:08:35,769
Click on a booth on the map.

164
00:08:35,769 --> 00:08:41,208
You can also see the booth's name, who the vendor is, and its specific column and row.

165
00:08:41,208 --> 00:08:42,818
Next, let's check out the application page.

166
00:08:42,818 --> 00:08:47,259
Here, the stall owner can fill in their name, email, phone number, and the stall's name.

167
00:08:47,259 --> 00:08:52,010
They can select the booth type and enter the required area.

168
00:08:52,010 --> 00:08:55,760
They can also check if they need access to electricity and water.

169
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:59,660
When I click submit, the front-end will call the back-end API.

170
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:01,900
and send this information to the server.

171
00:09:01,900 --> 00:09:04,400
The backend will then create a record for the stall owner,

172
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:06,920
and then create a new record for the stall application.

173
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:09,839
and set its status to 'Pending Approval'.

174
00:09:09,839 --> 00:09:13,410
Once submitted successfully, the application is added to the database.

175
00:09:13,410 --> 00:09:15,450
It's now waiting for the administrator to process it in the backend.

176
00:09:15,450 --> 00:09:17,450
Next, let's head into the admin dashboard.

177
00:09:17,450 --> 00:09:22,730
Log in with the admin password. When an application is first submitted, its status is 'Pending Approval'.

178
00:09:22,730 --> 00:09:26,870
After the admin approves it, the status changes to 'Pending Arrangement'.

179
00:09:26,870 --> 00:09:31,970
Once the administrator drags it onto the map and assigns a specific location,

180
00:09:31,970 --> 00:09:33,790
the status changes to 'Scheduled'.

181
00:09:33,790 --> 00:09:36,350
Qwen also generated the backend validation rules. For example,

182
00:09:36,350 --> 00:09:40,089
Two booths cannot be assigned to the same spot.

183
00:09:40,089 --> 00:09:44,750
Booths that require power must be located near a power source.

184
00:09:44,750 --> 00:09:48,230
and for booths that might be noisy, like game or exhibition stalls,

185
00:09:48,230 --> 00:09:50,070
and shouldn't be placed too close to the audience area.

186
00:09:50,070 --> 00:09:53,380
Plus, the backend also provides a CSV export function.

187
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:56,500
The exported spreadsheet contains all the information.

188
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:57,920
Thinking back to last September,

189
00:09:57,920 --> 00:10:04,630
Back then, I was using the most powerful open-source Chinese coding model to build an app that was way less functional than this one.

190
00:10:04,630 --> 00:10:07,020
It took 5 iterations to get it mostly right.

191
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:12,500
But now, 3.7-Max got it to this level with just two prompts.

192
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:15,640
And that second prompt was just to approve the plan and let Qwen get to work.

193
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:19,699
It finished all the TODOs in just over ten minutes.

194
00:10:19,699 --> 00:10:22,730
And that even included multiple testing stages.

195
00:10:22,730 --> 00:10:25,030
The final deliverables list was also very comprehensive.

196
00:10:25,030 --> 00:10:26,830
So, what about the code quality?

197
00:10:26,830 --> 00:10:29,550
But just watching a demo of the features might not give you the full picture.

198
00:10:29,550 --> 00:10:33,390
So I had GPT-5.5 xhigh give it a review.

199
00:10:33,390 --> 00:10:35,350
So, GPT's overall evaluation of this project is...

200
00:10:35,350 --> 00:10:39,470
For a demo prototype generated by a large AI model in just over ten minutes,

201
00:10:39,470 --> 00:10:40,960
The demo is a prototype.

202
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:42,260
The level of completion is quite good.

203
00:10:42,260 --> 00:10:43,520
So, if I had to rate it...

204
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:45,180
Demo completeness: a 7 out of 10.

205
00:10:45,180 --> 00:10:47,280
For production readiness, I'd give it a 3.5.

206
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:48,620
out of a possible ten.

207
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:51,800
GPT acknowledges that Qwen has achieved a complete functional loop.

208
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:57,239
The tech stack is well-chosen, and constraint validation has been abstracted into a separate module.

209
00:10:57,239 --> 00:10:58,819
It has a testing mindset.

210
00:10:58,819 --> 00:11:01,020
The UI is highly demonstrable.

211
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:06,720
The issues include the fact that the admin backend has almost no real back-end authorization.

212
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:12,610
There's no strict validation for things like email format, string length, or phone numbers.

213
00:11:12,610 --> 00:11:16,490
The CSV export has issues with data formatting and security vulnerabilities.

214
00:11:16,490 --> 00:11:19,150
And I basically agree with the issues GPT pointed out.

215
00:11:19,150 --> 00:11:21,610
and that's what led to these issues in the code.

216
00:11:21,610 --> 00:11:23,329
And I have to admit, I was also largely to blame.

217
00:11:23,329 --> 00:11:28,090
After it generated the plan, I didn't review it carefully and just had Qwen start coding right away.

218
00:11:28,090 --> 00:11:30,770
If the requirements you give it from the get-go are too broad,

219
00:11:30,770 --> 00:11:33,650
So naturally, that's going to affect the final outcome.

220
00:11:33,650 --> 00:11:37,610
So, my advice is, when you're actually using it, make sure to align the requirements with the model first.

221
00:11:37,610 --> 00:11:38,470
and then have it start coding.

222
00:11:38,470 --> 00:11:42,270
I also took a screenshot of the TODO list from Qwen's coding session and sent it to GPT.

223
00:11:42,270 --> 00:11:45,490
I asked it to evaluate whether these TODO items were properly completed.

224
00:11:45,490 --> 00:11:48,220
And GPT scored each item as well.

225
00:11:48,220 --> 00:11:50,800
So, let's see what its strengths are.

226
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:52,650
and which areas still need work.

227
00:11:52,650 --> 00:11:54,319
In actual programming,

228
00:11:54,319 --> 00:11:57,519
So, having multiple models review each other's work is still absolutely essential.

229
00:11:57,519 --> 00:11:59,499
Even a powerhouse like GPT-5.5

230
00:11:59,499 --> 00:12:03,180
and having 3.7-Max review the code during the process,

231
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:04,820
you can get some unexpected rewards.

232
00:12:04,820 --> 00:12:08,960
And that's all we have for Qwen3.7-Max today.

233
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:12,500
From my experience, the Qwen Max model has improved significantly.

234
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:18,520
